<section class="frame history" aria-labelledby="history-heading">
    <header class="history-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">SINCE 1857</p>
        <h2 id="history-heading">History &amp; introduction</h2>
        <p class="history-lede">A second line of defence to the Regular Army — raised from citizens with civilian lives, serving the Indian Army when the nation calls.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="history-body">
        <div>
            <p>The genesis of the Territorial Army in India can be traced back to the first war of Independence in <strong>1857</strong>, when a Volunteer Force was raised. In <strong>1917</strong> the Indian Defence Force Act was enacted. Under it, all universities were asked to provide contingents to the Defence Force. <strong>Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose</strong> joined the Calcutta University Corps of the Indian Defence Force in 1918 — training there helped him later in leading the Indian National Army in the struggle for freedom. <strong>Pt. Jawaharlal Nehru</strong> was part of the Allahabad University Corps. As a matter of interest, <strong>Mahatma Gandhi</strong> also joined the South Africa Volunteer Force in 1898 and 1905, serving as Sergeant Major with the Indian Ambulance Corps during the Boer War and Zulu Rebellion.</p>

            <p>The Indian Defence Force had two branches: the European Branch (the old Volunteer Force, rechristened the Auxiliary Force) and the Indian Branch, which later became the Indian Territorial Force. The Territorial Force was constituted for service within the limits of the Indian frontiers.</p>

            <p>Sir Charles Monroe, Commander-in-Chief of the Forces in India, introduced a bill in the Indian Legislative Council on <strong>27 August 1920</strong> to constitute an Indian Territorial Force, which was duly passed. Its aim: form a second line of defence to the Regular Army.</p>

            <p>After Independence the Territorial Army Act was passed in <strong>1948</strong>. The Territorial Army was formally inaugurated by the first Indian Governor General, <strong>Shri C. Rajagopalachari</strong>, on <strong>9 October 1949</strong>. This day is celebrated as <strong>TA Day</strong> every year.</p>

            <p>The Territorial Army initially had various types of units — Armoured Regiment (TA), Infantry Battalion (TA), Air Defence (TA), Medium Regiment (TA), Engineers Field Park Company (TA), Signal Regiment (TA), EME Workshop (TA), Coast Battery (TA), ASC GT Company (TA), ASC Compo Platoon (TA) and AMC Field Ambulance (TA). By 1972 these had either been disbanded or converted to the Regular Army, except Infantry Battalions (TA).</p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <p>Presently the Territorial Army has a strength of approximately <strong>fifty thousand personnel</strong>, comprising 65 Departmental TA units (such as Railway, IOC, ONGC) and Non-Departmental TA units: Infantry Battalions (TA) including Home &amp; Hearth Battalions, Ecological Battalions (TA) affiliated to various Infantry Regiments, and Engineer Regiments (TA) for the maintenance of Line-of-Control fencing. One Composite Eco Task Force for the National Mission for Clean Ganga is under raising at Allahabad.</p>

            <p>The Eco Task Force is dedicated to development, preservation and maintenance of ecological balance and afforestation in difficult areas. Ecological units have arrested man-made environmental degradation by planting approximately <strong>6 crore 65 lakh trees over 66,000 hectares</strong> — in the Mussoorie Hills and Pithoragarh (UK), Shimla (HP), Jaisalmer (Rajasthan), Bhatti Mines (Delhi), Charduar, Kokrajhar (Assam) and Samba (J&amp;K).</p>

            <p>TA units have been actively involved in the <strong>1962, 1965 and 1971 operations</strong>. The Terriers have taken part in Op Pawan in Sri Lanka, Op Rakshak in Punjab and J&amp;K, and Op Rhino and Op Bajrang in the North-East. Departmental units have come to the aid of civil authorities during industrial unrest and natural calamities.</p>

            <p>The Territorial Army is a part of the Regular Army. Its present role: relieve the Regular Army from static duties; assist civil administration in dealing with natural calamities and in the maintenance of essential services in situations where life of the communities is affected or the security of the country is threatened; and provide units for the Regular Army as and when required.</p>

            <p>Men of the TA have taken part in various adventure activities — a famous one being the scaling of <strong>Mt. Tengchen Khang (6,010 m)</strong> in West Sikkim by the Joint Indo-British TA Mountaineering Expedition in May 1998.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="history-honours">
        <h3>Gallantry &amp; distinguished service</h3>
        <p>So far, officers and men of the Territorial Army have earned:</p>
        <ul class="honours-grid">
            <li><b>01</b> Kirti Chakra</li>
            <li><b>05</b> Ati Vishisht Seva Medal</li>
            <li><b>05</b> Vir Chakra</li>
            <li><b>05</b> Shourya Chakra</li>
            <li><b>01</b> Yudh Seva Medal</li>
            <li><b>74</b> Sena Medal</li>
            <li><b>28</b> Vishisht Seva Medal</li>
            <li><b>17</b> Mention-in-Dispatches</li>
            <li><b>266</b> Chief of the Army Staff Commendation Cards</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fine">Many industrialists, politicians and eminent personalities have been members of the TA. The force has had the honour of appointing 11 officers over the years as Hony. ADC to the President of India.</p>
    </aside>

    <div class="history-timeline">
        <h3>Evolution of TA in the Indian context</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><time>1857</time><p>Raised as “Volunteers”, recruited only from Europeans &amp; Anglo-Indians.</p></li>
            <li><time>Oct 1920</time><p>Indian Territorial Force Bill passed; TA reorganised into two wings — the Auxiliary Force and the Indian Territorial Force.</p></li>
            <li><time>18 Aug 1948</time><p>Territorial Army Act enacted by the Constituent Assembly, constituting the present Territorial Army.</p></li>
            <li><time>9 Oct 1949</time><p>First TA camp formally inaugurated by Shri C. Rajagopalachari. Celebrated as TA Day every year.</p></li>
            <li><time>1951 – 1968</time><p>Fourteen Infantry TA units raised; 51 Lt Armd Regt re-designated as 122 Inf Bn (TA) MADRAS.</p></li>
            <li><time>19 Oct 1968</time><p>Six GH (TA) raised; later disbanded on 13 Feb 2014.</p></li>
            <li><time>1977</time><p>Approx. 16 units of AD Regt (TA) along with their AD Workshops converted to the Regular Army.</p></li>
            <li><time>1982</time><p>First Inf Bn (TA) Eco — 127 Inf Bn (TA) Eco GARH RIF — raised at Dehradun.</p></li>
            <li><time>11 Dec 1990</time><p>777 Sig Regt (TA) raised; later disbanded.</p></li>
            <li><time>1994</time><p>TA Day Parade designated as Prime Minister’s Territorial Army Day Parade.</p></li>
            <li><time>May 1999</time><p>Ministry of Railways disbanded three Rly Engr Regt (TA).</p></li>
            <li><time>2001</time><p>Rly Engr Regt (TA) and Bridge Construction Coys paid by MoD converted into Inf Bns (TA) — 150 to 155 Inf Bns (TA).</p></li>
            <li><time>2004 – 2005</time><p>Eleven Home &amp; Hearth (TA) units raised — J&amp;K (08), NE (03).</p></li>
            <li><time>2015</time><p>Three Engr Regt (TA) raised for repairs of LC fence in 15 &amp; 16 Corps zones.</p></li>
            <li><time>2017</time><p>Footprint expanded: 172 Inf Bn (TA) MADRAS raised at A&amp;N Islands; 136 Inf Bn (TA) Eco MAHAR raised for drought-hit Marathwada, Maharashtra.</p></li>
            <li><time>2018</time><p>37 CETF Bn (TA) 39 GR being raised for the National Mission for Clean Ganga.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>
